[info]   Rules:
[info]   1. schemaVersion is "synthesis-output-v3". At most 20 claims, with claimId "claim-1", "claim-2", ... in order.
[info]   2. Answer only from the bundle. Every claim other than DESCRIPTIVE cites the evidence IDs (the id attribute of an <item>) that support it and has at least one values entry. values lists each fact the claim asserts under the exact fact name of a cited item, with the value copied exactly as a JSON string (for example "distinctCoauthorCount": "142"). Omit any claim you cannot cite with at least one evidence ID; never leave evidenceIds empty. Keep each claim concise: one sentence that states only the facts in its values.
[info]   3. Every number written in a claim (counts, measures, sizes, years, ranks) is in values under the fact name it comes from, and the text names that measure next to the number (for example "142 distinct coauthors", "weighted degree 3"). Copy numbers; never compute, round, estimate, or combine them. Write a decimal with all of its digits as stored. Derived values (for example maximumTeamSize, which is maximumCoauthorsOnOnePaper + 1 authors) are already in the facts.
[info]   4. Set window to the window fact of the cited items for every claim about a metric, ranking, edge, path, component, community, bridge measure, facet, or change. A window name or year range written in the text must be that window (a change claim may name its two windows). First and last collaboration years are facts in values, not windows. subjectIds lists the researcher IDs the claim is about.
[info]   5. Choose kind: IDENTITY (cites IDENTITY_RESOLUTION), METRIC_VALUE (METRIC_RECORD, RANKING_RECORD, or TEMPORAL_CHANGE), RANKING (RANKING_RECORD), COAUTHORSHIP (COAUTHOR_EDGE), PATH and PATH_HOP (GRAPH_PATH), COMPONENT, COMMUNITY, BRIDGE (values name a bridge measure), TEMPORAL_CHANGE, PUBLICATION (WORK_RECORD), FACET (FACET_RECORD), DESCRIPTIVE (semantic context only). A PATH_HOP claim puts hop.<k>.from, hop.<k>.to, hop.<k>.jointPublicationCount, hop.<k>.firstYear, and hop.<k>.lastYear in values. A rank number appears only in a RANKING claim whose values hold rank.<k>.storedRank and rank.<k>.researcherId of that researcher. Whenever values hold any rank.<k> fact (rank.<k>.value, rank.<k>.storedRank, rank.<k>.displayName, ...), they also hold rank.<k>.researcherId, in every kind of claim.
[info]   6. Name the measure you report and keep them apart when the question touches them: collaboration breadth is distinct coauthors; intensity is weighted degree (joint publications summed over coauthors); team size is the largest number of authors on one paper; connectivity is the connected component; bridge importance is cross-community degree, communities connected, and participation coefficient; coreness is the k-core. High breadth is not high bridge importance.
[info]   7. Semantic context (<semantic_context>) is descriptive only. Never cite it for a number, a coauthorship, a path, a component, a community, or a bridge value; such claims cite graph evidence. A DESCRIPTIVE claim has no number, names at most one researcher, and states no count, relationship, path, component, community, or bridge measure; it is shown as context only.
[info]   8. Use double quotes inside a claim text only around a paper title copied exactly from a cited WORK_RECORD.
[info]   9. Use only researcher names that appear in the bundle, written exactly as there. Never write the name of any other person, and never guess an identity.
[info]   10. Put in uncertainty every note from <notes> that applies (window choice, identity links, missing evidence), and say what the bundle does not contain. Do not invent missing facts.
